{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792497224,"nonce":"fN1Z3I/Ku70L7MNRhkWKU04vRPE=","title":"TypeScript: lower dense typed arrays with GEPs and cached generic instances","message":"Connect TypeScript array types and dense literals to the shared growable-array representation. Preserve typed GEP/load/store indexing, shared inline list operations and static drop analysis. Support nested generic array parameters/returns and verify cached monomorphization for native numeric, boolean and string element types without dynamic boxing. TypeScript number remains f64; integer storage is a distinct compiler specialization, not an implicit change to number semantics.\n\nCover array identity, lifetime and evaluation order across interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM, including optimized HIR after inlining/SROA. In-bounds access must be proven; reads that can yield undefined, sparse extension and reference ownership not yet proven must remain explicit Unsupported gaps rather than unsafe loads or altered JavaScript results. String elements initially require proven immortal storage until reference-bearing container ownership is implemented.\n\nParent: 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5","files":null}]}